tion","tactics":["Persistence","Privilege Escalation"],"behavior":"PowerShell accessed HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\ControlSet001\\Control\\Lsa registry key multiple times to enumerate or modify LSA security policies and credential settings.","slot":0,"reasoning":"The evidence shows PowerShell and mshta.exe accessing HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\ControlSet001\\Control\\Lsa with read access (0x1), which is the registry location for Security Support Provider configuration. This pattern matches SSP persistence abuse rather than LSA Secrets dumping (which targets HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SECURITY\\Policy\\Secrets) or LSASS memory access.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E031","field":"ObjectName","quote":"\\REGISTRY\\MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\ControlSet001\\Control\\Lsa","ok":true},{"event_id":"E031","field":"ProcessName","quote":"C:\\Windows\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe","ok":true},{"event_id":"E006","field":"ProcessName","quote":"C:\\Windows\\System32\\mshta.exe","ok":true}],"attempts":1},{"technique_id":"T1134.005","name":"SID-History Injection","parent":"Access Token Manipulation","tactics":["Stealth","Privilege Escalation"],"behavior":"PowerShell invoked SeTcbPrivilege and SeCreateGlobalPrivilege to attempt privilege escalation or system-level operations.","slot":1,"reasoning":"The events show PowerShell requesting SeTcbPrivilege and SeCreateGlobalPrivilege via privileged service calls (Event 4673), which indicates token manipulation to elevate privileges. This is characteristic of token impersonation/elevation rather than exploitation of a vulnerability or persistence mechanism. T1134.005 (Access Token Manipulation: Token Impersonation/Theft) best describes the technique of acquiring and using elevated tokens for privilege escalation.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E038","field":"PrivilegeList","quote":"SeTcbPrivilege","ok":true},{"event_id":"E040","field":"PrivilegeList","quote":"SeCreateGlobalPrivilege","ok":true},{"event_id":"E038","field":"ProcessName","quote":"C:\\Windows\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe","ok":true}],"attempts":1},{"technique_id":"T1553.004","name":"Install Root Certificate","parent":"Subvert Trust Controls","tactics":["Defense Impairment"],"behavior":"mshta.exe and PowerShell modified Internet Settings ZoneMap registry to configure proxy bypass and intranet settings, and accessed certificate stores (ROOT, Policies, Enterprise).","slot":2,"reasoning":"The evidence shows PowerShell accessing ROOT, Policies, and Enterprise certificate stores in the registry\u2014the exact locations where root certificates are installed and managed. While mshta.exe modified proxy settings (a separate defensive measure), the primary adversary objective here is installing or manipulating root certificates to subvert trust controls and avoid warnings when connecting to attacker-controlled servers.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E044","field":"ObjectName","quote":"\\REGISTRY\\MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\SystemCertificates\\ROOT\\Certificates","ok":true},{"event_id":"E045","field":"ObjectName","quote":"\\REGISTRY\\MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\SystemCertificates\\Root\\Certificates","ok":true},{"event_id":"E046","field":"ObjectName","quote":"\\REGISTRY\\MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\EnterpriseCertificates\\Root\\Certificates","ok":true}],"attempts":1}],"unverified":[],"declined":[],"hypotheses":[{"behavior":"PowerShell accessed HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\ControlSet001\\Control\\Lsa registry key multiple times to enumerate or modify LSA security policies and credential settings.","event_ids":["E031","E032","E006","E007"],"keywords":["LSA","registry access","credential access","security policies","HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\Control\\Lsa"]},{"behavior":"PowerShell invoked SeTcbPrivilege and SeCreateGlobalPrivilege to attempt privilege escalation or system-level operations.","event_ids":["E038","E040"],"keywords":["privilege escalation","SeTcbPrivilege","SeCreateGlobalPrivilege","privileged service call"]},{"be